    "available_at": "2026-11-19T10:00:29.819105+00:00",
    "body": "There’s a party going on at DISCO Pharma’s European headquarters after the precision medicine biotech signed its second major pharma partnership of the year—first Amgen, now AbbVie.\nAbbVie has found some hot stuff in DISCO Pharmaceuticals’ logic-based bispecific platform and has struck a deal to the tune of $2.54 billion in biobucks for an option to develop solid tumor therapies.\nThe Big Pharma is going low with a $40 million upfront payment for the European biotech and high on the backend with milestones worth up to $2.5 billion.\nThe release was otherwise slim on details of the collaboration but said the focus will be on developing novel AND-gated bispecific therapeutics for solid tumors with high unmet medical need. These antibodies must recognize two distinct antigens simultaneously to become fully active.\nThe companies are planning a celebration to last throughout the years, with DISCO Chief Business Officer Ajla Hrle noting that the partnership shines the light on the biotech’s DISCOtech platform. The technology maps novel cell surface targets to develop targeted therapies such as antibody-drug conjugates and T cell engagers.\nDISCO boogied onto 2026’s dance floor in January by signing a licensing deal with Amgen worth up to $618 million in total deal value.\nAbbVie is staying alive in pharma’s ongoing deal frenzy, with executives promising investors during third quarter earnings last month that they have plenty of capacity for M&A .\nOn the licensing front, AbbVie most recently asked Nxera Pharma to dance, offering $100 million upfront plus $1.37 billion on the back end for an EP4 receptor agonist program in inflammatory bowel disease.\nMergers & acquisitions\n5 acquisitions that forced Big Pharmas to co-parent\nAcquiring a biotech with an existing Big Pharma partner can impose financial and strategic constraints around data sharing and milestone and royalty payments—but it is “rarely a deal-breaker,” according to one biotech consultant.\nNovember 18, 2026\n·\n8 min read\n·\nTristan Manalac\nRead more",
